          "id": "jr2012-q13",
          "type": "mc",
          "stem": "If \\(f(x) = x^2-9x+14\\), then the asymptotes of the curve with equation \\(y=\\dfrac{1}{f(x)}\\) are:",
          "options": [
            "\\(x=2, x=7\\) and \\(y=\\dfrac{1}{14}\\)",
            "\\(x=2, x=7\\) and \\(y=0\\)",
            "\\(x=-2, x=-7\\) and \\(y=\\dfrac{1}{14}\\)",
            "\\(x=-2, x=-7\\) and \\(y=0\\)"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "hint": "Vertical asymptotes occur where \\(f(x)=0\\) (the denominator vanishes); the horizontal asymptote is found from the behaviour of \\(\\frac1{f(x)}\\) as \\(x\\to\\pm\\infty\\).",
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Factorise \\(f(x)\\) to find where it equals zero — these give the vertical asymptotes of \\(y=1/f(x)\\).",
              "work": "\\(f(x) = (x-2)(x-7) = 0 \\Rightarrow x=2, x=7\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "As \\(x\\to\\pm\\infty\\), \\(f(x)\\to\\infty\\), so \\(\\dfrac{1}{f(x)}\\to 0\\) — this gives the horizontal asymptote.",
              "work": "As \\(x\\to\\pm\\infty\\), \\(y\\to 0\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Combine both results.",
              "work": "Asymptotes: \\(x=2, x=7, y=0\\)"
            }
          ],
          "meta": {
            "source": "James Ruse AHS 2012 Year 10 Yearly",
